# E4 scripted: environments & promotion.
# No Spark server is involved — every endpoint declared in build.sbt is
# deliberately dead. What is under test is the TARGET MODEL: that a bad target
# name fails usefully, that a `*On` task really dials its target's endpoint, and
# that the manifest is independent of the environment (the promotion property).

# --- (a) addressing errors, before any network. The message TEXT is asserted in
# the unit specs (SdpTargetSpec / TargetResolutionSpec); here we assert they
# FAIL, which is what a build actually depends on.

# an undeclared target name
-> sdpRunOn nope
-> sdpDryRunOn nope
-> sdpSeedOn nope

# no target name at all
-> sdpDryRunOn

# a target whose tokenEnv is not exported: refused at resolution, not at the socket
-> sdpDryRunOn prod-secure

# --- (b) the target's endpoint is the one actually dialled: the failure names
# prod's endpoint and provenance, and never the flat setting's (which points at
# a different dead endpoint, so this cannot pass by accident).
> checkProdEndpointUsed

# --- (c) THE PROMOTION PROPERTY — one manifest, every environment.
> sdpManifest
> manifestSnapshot

# Registering against dev is a network failure (dead endpoint) — the manifest
# behind it is the same artifact either way.
-> sdpDryRunOn dev
> sdpManifest
> checkManifestIdentical

# A completely different environment: other endpoint, other catalog, other
# database, other storage root.
-> sdpDryRunOn prod
> sdpManifest
> checkManifestIdentical

# And now move the WHOLE build to a different environment — flat settings and
# every target value — without touching a single pipeline source.
$ copy-file extra/promoted-build.sbt build.sbt
> reload
> checkPromotedSettings
> sdpManifest
> checkManifestIdentical

# The untargeted tasks still behave exactly as before (flat settings), and the
# offline ones never touch a target at all — including after the environment
# moved under them.
> sdpValidate
> sdpManifest
> checkManifestIdentical
-> sdpDryRun
